<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Middle Juz</h1><p class="st-meta">1718 CE – 1822 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The central division of the Kazakhs, whose khan Ablai unified much of the steppe before Russia absorbed it in 1822.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tauke-khan">Tauke Khan's</a> death around 1718 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate</a> split into three hordes, with the Middle Juz holding the central and north-eastern steppe from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh</a>. Its main tribes included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyn">Argyn</a>, Naiman, Kerei and Kypchak.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Middle Juz fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a>, and in the 1740s its khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abilmambet">Abilmambet</a> and the sultan Ablai accepted Russian protection while balancing ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing</a>. Ablai, who became khan in 1771, defeated rivals and dominated much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakh</a> steppe. The biy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazybek-bi">Kazybek Bi</a> was among its noted judges and diplomats.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Ablai's death in 1781 and the death of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vali-khan">Vali Khan</a> in 1819, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-speransky">Speransky</a> reforms of 1822 abolished the khanship and organised the territory into Russian districts. Ablai's grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenesary-kasymov">Kenesary Kasymov</a> later led a revolt against Russian rule.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/middle-juz.jpg" alt="Middle Juz" width="320" height="180"><figcaption>Image: ru:User:Wassily · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%96%D1%83%D0%B7.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1718 CE – 1822 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abilmambet, Ablai Khan, Vali Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kazakh</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Kazakhstan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Middle Horde, Orta Juz</dd></dl></aside></div>
